// D-05-02 tracer fixture: touch's sole Buffer parameter is exclusively
// borrowed as the very FIRST use in the body (`borrow mut buffer`) and never
// referenced again directly -- every remaining operation is a reborrow
// chained off that exclusive loan (a repeated load/store over the borrowed
// view an optimizer could hoist, the seed of D-05-05's later engineered
// fixture), so the loan's own last use is the function's own terminator.
// This is the structural shape selectsByPointerLowering (cgen.go) detects:
// exclusive loan on the parameter covering every operation from the
// parameter's first use to the terminator, never a fixture-name allowlist.
//
// The return type is a PLAIN owned `-> Buffer`, not a declared
// `borrow(...)` view: selectsByPointerLowering additionally requires
// function.PublicOrigin == nil, so a declared borrow-return function (e.g.
// testdata/phase3/public_view_mixed_access.lang, which has the identical
// exclusive-borrow-then-reborrow-to-terminator operation shape) never
// selects this path -- PublicOrigin is a genuinely distinct, already-shipped
// semantic category this plan does not touch. check.go admits a plain
// return of a borrow-derived place unchanged (no checker change this plan;
// this is the documented D-03-02 gap, not a new behavior).
module phase5.restrict_borrow

export {
  fn touch
}

fn touch(buffer: Buffer) -> Buffer {
  let first = borrow mut buffer
  let second = borrow first
  let third = borrow second
  third
}
